# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# FlowBoard — environment template
#
# Copy this file to `.env` at the repo root and adjust. `.env` is git-ignored;
# this example is the only version that is committed, so keep it in sync with
# `apps/api/src/config/env.ts` (the zod schema that fails fast on boot).
#
#   cp .env.example .env        # macOS / Linux / Git Bash
#   Copy-Item .env.example .env # PowerShell
#
# Defaults below match `docker-compose.dev.yml`, so with the dev compose up the
# file works as-is.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Runtime ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `development` | `test` | `production`. Guards dev-only behaviour (verbose
# errors, permissive CORS logging). Never ship `development` to a real deploy.
NODE_ENV=development

# Port the Express API listens on. The web dev server proxies /api and
# /socket.io here (see apps/web/vite.config.ts).
PORT=4000

# Exact origin of the web app, used for CORS and the Socket.IO handshake check.
# Must include the scheme and port, and must NOT have a trailing slash.
WEB_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# pino log level: fatal | error | warn | info | debug | trace.
# Also the floor for the in-memory ring buffer behind the diagnostics drawer.
LOG_LEVEL=debug

# ── Database (Postgres 17) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Full connection URL consumed by postgres-js and drizzle-kit. The dev compose
# publishes Postgres on localhost:5432 with these credentials.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/flowboard

# Host port the dev compose publishes Postgres on. Only set this if something
# else already owns 5432 (another project's Postgres container is the usual
# culprit; the symptom is `Bind for 0.0.0.0:5432 failed: port is already
# allocated`). If you change it, change the port in DATABASE_URL to match.
# POSTGRES_PORT=5433

# ── Auth (JWT) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TWO DIFFERENT secrets — a refresh token must never verify as an access token.
# Generate real ones for anything non-local:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
JWT_SECRET=dev-access-secret-change-me
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=dev-refresh-secret-change-me

# Token lifetimes, in `ms`-package syntax (e.g. 15m, 2h, 30d).
# Access tokens are short-lived; the web client silently single-flight refreshes.
ACCESS_TOKEN_TTL=15m
REFRESH_TOKEN_TTL=30d

# ── Object storage (MinIO, S3-compatible) ────────────────────────────────────
# Task attachments are uploaded straight from the browser via presigned URLs.
# In dev this is the MinIO container from docker-compose.dev.yml; in production
# point it at any S3-compatible endpoint.
S3_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
S3_ACCESS_KEY=flowboard
S3_SECRET_KEY=flowboard-dev-secret
S3_BUCKET=flowboard-attachments
S3_REGION=us-east-1

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# PRODUCTION — `docker-compose.yml`
#
# Everything above this line configures a DEVELOPMENT machine, where the API and
# the web app run natively against `docker-compose.dev.yml`. The production
# stack (`docker-compose.yml`) runs all five services in containers and reads
# the variables BELOW instead.
#
# Compose does not merge two env files. On a machine that also does development,
# keep production values in their own file and name it explicitly, so the dev
# `.env` above is never read by the production stack:
#
#   docker compose --env-file .env.production up -d
#
# On a real server there is no dev stack, so a plain `.env` holding only this
# block is the normal arrangement.
#
# Compose marks the four secrets below as REQUIRED (`${VAR:?…}`): it refuses to
# start and names the missing variable, rather than booting with an example
# value that would sign forgeable tokens. Everything else has a working default.
#
# Full walkthrough: docs/docker-guide.md § Running the production stack.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── MUST be changed before a real deployment ────────────────────────────────
# Generate each secret separately — never reuse one value in two places:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(48).toString('base64url'))"
#
# JWT_SECRET / JWT_REFRESH_SECRET are the same two variables as above; the
# production stack simply refuses the `dev-…-change-me` placeholders' intent by
# requiring you to set them. A refresh token must never verify as an access
# token, so the two values must DIFFER.
#
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD becomes part of a URL. If it contains any of `@ : / ? #`,
# percent-encode it — `postgres-js` parses DATABASE_URL as a URL and will
# otherwise read the password as a hostname.
#
# S3_SECRET_KEY doubles as the MinIO root password; the compose file wires the
# same value into both the storage service and the API.
#
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=<generate>
# JWT_SECRET=<generate>
# JWT_REFRESH_SECRET=<generate — different from JWT_SECRET>
# S3_SECRET_KEY=<generate>

# ── WEB_ORIGIN — the one that silently breaks everything ────────────────────
# The exact origin the BROWSER loads the app from: scheme + host + port, no
# trailing slash. It is the CORS allow-list for REST *and* the Socket.IO
# handshake check. Get it wrong and the login page renders perfectly, then every
# request fails with an opaque network error and the board never goes live.
#
#   local verification            : http://localhost:8080
#   behind a TLS terminator       : https://flowboard.example.com
#
# WEB_ORIGIN=http://localhost:8080

# ── NODE_ENV ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Do NOT set this for the production stack. `docker-compose.yml` hard-codes
# `NODE_ENV=production` on the api and migrate services precisely so that a
# stale `NODE_ENV=development` sitting in an env file cannot downgrade a
# deployment. The value above is for the native dev processes only.

# ── Host ports published by the production stack ────────────────────────────
# Chosen to stay clear of the dev stack (5173 Vite, 4000 API, 5432 Postgres,
# 9000/9001 MinIO) so both can run side by side on one machine.
#
# WEB_PORT=8080            # nginx — the only port users need
# S3_PORT=9200             # MinIO S3 API; the browser uploads directly to it
# S3_CONSOLE_PORT=9201     # MinIO admin console
# S3_CONSOLE_BIND=127.0.0.1  # loopback only; use an SSH tunnel on a server

# ── Object storage addressing ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# An S3 signature covers the host and port, and the API signs URLs that the
# BROWSER dials — so one address has to resolve from inside the compose network
# and from the user's machine, on the same port. `docker-compose.yml` publishes
# S3_PUBLIC_HOST as a network alias on the minio service to make that true.
#
# The default works locally with no DNS setup, because browsers resolve any
# `*.localhost` name to loopback (RFC 6761). For a real deployment point it at
# the storage hostname your users can reach and terminate TLS in front of it.
#
# S3_PUBLIC_HOST=flowboard.localhost
# S3_SCHEME=http           # https once a TLS terminator fronts MinIO

# ── Database and object-storage names ───────────────────────────────────────
# The production stack builds DATABASE_URL from these three; it ignores the
# DATABASE_URL above, which points at a host port that does not exist inside a
# container.
#
# POSTGRES_USER=flowboard
# POSTGRES_DB=flowboard
# S3_ACCESS_KEY=flowboard  # doubles as the MinIO root user
# S3_BUCKET=flowboard-attachments
# S3_REGION=us-east-1

# ── Optional ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LOG_LEVEL=info           # `debug` is far too chatty for a real deployment
# ACCESS_TOKEN_TTL=15m
# REFRESH_TOKEN_TTL=30d
